fuxi: 0116 -- name a huge kmem_alloc before it kills the phone

The last boot died with:

  panic(cpu 0 caller ...): kmem_alloc(0xffffffe100161c830, 45000760, 0x100021):
    failed with 3 @vm_kern.c:178

0x45000760 is 1.08 GiB in one request.  kmem_alloc_guard_internal() refuses an
extent that big (the wired-extent limit scales with RAM, which is 3 GiB here) and
__kmem_failed_panic() panics with the size and nothing else -- no caller, no tag --
so the ring shows a healthy boot followed by a dead phone and no way to tell which
subsystem asked.

This prints the size, the flags and three return addresses for anything at or above
32 MiB, before the limit is evaluated, so the next boot's console says who asked.
Diagnostic only: allocation behaviour is untouched.

Harness: applied=115 skipped=0.
--- a/osfmk/vm/vm_kern.c
+++ b/osfmk/vm/vm_kern.c
@@ -789,6 +789,19 @@
 	}
 
 	/*
+	 * A single huge kmem_alloc is how this port dies: the wired-extent limit
+	 * below refuses it and __kmem_failed_panic() panics with the size and nothing
+	 * else, so the ring shows a healthy boot and then a dead phone.  Name the
+	 * request and its callers while a console still exists to print to.
+	 */
+	if (__improbable(size >= (32ULL << 20))) {
+		kprintf("FUXI ALLOC: %llu bytes flags 0x%x caller %p <- %p <- %p\n",
+		    (uint64_t)size, (uint32_t)flags,
+		    __builtin_return_address(0), __builtin_return_address(1),
+		    __builtin_return_address(2));
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * limit the size of a single extent of wired memory
 	 * to try and limit the damage to the system if
 	 * too many pages get wired down
